# Credentials for scripts/shopify-data. Copy to .env and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Never paste these values into a chat, a PR, or an issue.
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED — it must never contain a real value.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A CUSTOM APP AND NOT A THEME ACCESS TOKEN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Theme Access tokens cover theme FILES only. Metafield definitions and
# collections are store data, not theme files, so they need the Admin API.
#
# Legacy custom apps handed out a static token from a page in the Shopify admin.
# Shopify closed those to merchants on 1 Jan 2026. The token now comes from a
# Dev Dashboard app via a ONE-TIME OAuth exchange.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USE A DEVELOPMENT STORE, NOT A TRIAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A trial store created at shopify.com/signup expires after its trial and then
# answers every Admin API call with `402 Payment Required — Unavailable Shop`.
# That is what happened to ffaznr-4y in September 2026.
#
# A DEVELOPMENT store, created from the Partner/Dev Dashboard organisation, is
# free, does not expire, and is the correct home for this work. It cannot take
# real payments — which is right: Girlyf pick a plan when the store is
# transferred to them at launch, so we never own their billing.
#
# A frozen trial CANNOT be converted into a development store. The move is a new
# store plus a re-apply, which is cheap here because the store's configuration is
# code:  npm run data:apply   →   npm run demo:seed   →   npm run push
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO CREATE IT  (about ten minutes, once)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. dev.shopify.com → your organisation → Apps → Create app
#      Name it something recognisable, e.g. "Girlyf store data".
#
#   2. Distribution → Custom distribution → the store this repo targets.
#      Custom distribution locks the app to one store. That is what you want —
#      it is not going on the App Store.
#
#      The choice of custom-vs-public is PERMANENT, and a custom app is bound to
#      one store, OR to the stores of one organisation if it was created inside
#      one. The homingo app covers several stores in Crownshire Elevate LLP for
#      exactly that reason.
#
#      So before building a second app: check whether the new store sits in the
#      same organisation as this one. If it does, install the existing app on it
#      from Overview -> Distribution and skip steps 1 to 5 entirely.
#
#   3. Configuration → allowed redirection URL:
#        http://localhost:3456/callback
#
#   4. Configuration → Admin API access scopes:
#        read_products, write_products      — products and collections
#        write_publications                 — publishing to the Online Store
#        read_inventory, write_inventory    — stock
#        write_files                        — the one-off launch media migration
#        read_locations                     — stock cannot be set without a location id
#        read_product_listings              — reading whether a product is published
#
#      Metafield DEFINITIONS are authorised by their owner resource, which is
#      why write_products is what covers product and collection metafields.
#      There is no separate metafield scope to add.
#
#   5. Release a new version. Configuration changes do NOTHING until released.
#      This is the step everyone forgets, and the failure looks like a
#      permissions bug rather than an unreleased change.
#
#   6. Put the client id and secret below, then run:
#        npm run data:authorize
#
#      That prints an OFFLINE access token, which does not expire. Paste it into
#      SHOPIFY_ADMIN_TOKEN and you never run the exchange again.
#
# CHANGING SCOPES LATER means releasing a new version AND re-running
# data:authorize. An existing token keeps the scopes it was granted with — it
# does not pick up new ones, and again the failure looks like a permissions bug.

# Must match `config.store` in package.json — that is the canonical name, and
# the Admin-API write guard refuses to run against anything else.
SHOPIFY_STORE=ffaznr-4y.myshopify.com

# OAuth pair from the app's Configuration page. Used ONLY by data:authorize.
# The secret is a credential in its own right — rotate it if it ever leaks.
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Printed by `npm run data:authorize`. Offline token, does not expire.
# This is what apply.mjs actually authenticates with.
SHOPIFY_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Check this against the version dropdown in the store's admin before bumping.
# A version that does not exist 404s the entire run.
SHOPIFY_API_VERSION=2026-07
